1
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:12,262
Compost is what happens when microbes eat our food and garden waste.

2
00:00:12,412 --> 00:00:19,537
Bacteria and fungi break down carbon-rich browns and nitrogen-rich greens, ideally mixed about 25–30 : 1.

3
00:00:19,687 --> 00:00:26,050
With oxygen, they turn it into CO₂, water, heat and humus, the dark, stable part of good soil.

4
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:33,657
Microbes can work with oxygen (aerobic) or without it (anaerobic). The difference matters.

5
00:00:33,807 --> 00:00:40,013
Aerobic: fast, hot (55–70 °C) and almost odourless. Carbon leaves as CO₂ and most nitrogen stays in the compost.

6
00:00:40,163 --> 00:00:47,050
Anaerobic: slow and cool. A wet, airless pile gives off methane, about 28× as warming as CO₂, and loses nitrogen as ammonia.

7
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:55,275
Meet Compostito: a home composter that runs each phase in its own chamber, stacked on stilts.

8
00:00:55,425 --> 00:01:02,478
Waste goes in at the top. Mesh walls and two vents let air rise through the pile: cool air in low, warm air out high.

9
00:01:02,628 --> 00:01:10,050
Every week or so, material moves down a chamber: A → B → C → D. C and D are half size, because the pile shrinks by 20–50 %.

10
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:17,305
As the microbes feed, they multiply, and their metabolism releases heat.

11
00:01:17,455 --> 00:01:22,918
Every kilogram of organic matter they break down releases about 18 MJ, roughly 5 kWh.

12
00:01:23,068 --> 00:01:30,050
The pile insulates itself, so heat builds up faster than it escapes: from 15 °C to over 60 °C in a few days.

13
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:35,999
That rise and fall gives the four phases of composting.

14
00:01:36,149 --> 00:01:43,838
Mesophilic (A), then thermophilic (B, 55–70 °C): heat-loving microbes take over, and the heat kills pathogens and weed seeds.

15
00:01:43,988 --> 00:01:51,050
Cooling (C) and maturation (D): fungi and slower microbes finish the job. Each turning brings fresh oxygen back in.

16
00:01:53,700 --> 00:01:58,418
In chamber B, water flows through an aluminium coil and picks up the heat.

17
00:01:58,568 --> 00:02:06,445
But compost passes heat on poorly: 99.5 % of the resistance is on the compost side, so a buried pipe gets only about 50 W.

18
00:02:06,595 --> 00:02:16,050
Scale fixes it. On a farm, fans push air through the pile, and an air-to-water exchanger sends the heat to dairy hot water, barns and greenhouses.

19
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:23,995
After a few weeks of maturation, what comes out of chamber D is dark, crumbly compost.

20
00:02:24,145 --> 00:02:33,050
Because it was made with oxygen, it is stable and sanitised, holds its nutrients, and adds organic matter that keeps water and feeds soil life.
